content.text:
Yes. Per spec (`15_semantics.rst`, “Overriding in Classes/Interfaces”), a single subclass/subinterface method may override several superclass/superinterface methods.

In this PR’s code, that case should pass: `CheckMultipleOverride` only errors when a later overload’s `OverridesSignatures()` intersects with signatures already overridden by another method in the same overload set. If one method overrides multiple signatures by itself, no error is emitted.

I also validated with a temp file:

```ets
class A {}
class B {}
class Base {
  foo(a: A): void {}
  foo(b: B): void {}
}
class Derived extends Base {
  foo(o: Object): void {}
}
```

`es2panda` exited `0`.

Caveat: the new test’s “a method in a subclass overrides multiple methods” case is actually a negative case because it adds a second overload that also overrides one of the same base methods. It doesn’t cover the pure positive case above.
